session:pr701Summary a agp:TranscriptSummary ;
    agp:summaryText "Issue #683 was filed as a follow-up during #679/PR #682: negation_invalidated_seeds scanned every entry in a program's whole derived_from index per candidate (rule_id, NOT-pattern) match -- O(new facts x total derived facts across all programs) -- since no rule_id -> derivations reverse index existed, unlike the witness-quad reverse index apply_deletions already maintains. Read #682's PR body and provenance summary first per the task brief, confirming the design constraints: neg_rule_map, reconstruct_substitution and cascade_pd all had to keep working unmodified. Added a by_rule: HashMap<usize, HashMap<Quad,usize>> field to DerivedFromIndex (datalog/src/types.rs), maintained incrementally inside record/unrecord/remove (no new call sites needed) rather than as a separately-rebuilt index, directly extending the dedup-aware bookkeeping pattern record/unrecord already used for the forward index (#320) -- a per-quad count rather than a plain HashSet because the same quad can be derived twice by the same rule with different witnesses, and unrecord must not drop it from the reverse index while one derivation still remains. negation_invalidated_seeds then calls the new quads_for_rule(rule_id) instead of derived_from.iter(), narrowing the scan to exactly the derivations the matching rule could have produced. The task brief required a benchmark demonstrating the actual cost before trusting the fix, not just a structural diff. First attempt built the benchmark through the full IncrementalReasoner::new -> apply_insertions path with many 'noise' rule pairs engineered (via a vacuous NOT edge) to land in the same stratifier-assigned stratum as the negated rule; at NOISE=2500 (the largest feasible before RulePartitioner::new's own O(rules^2) dependency-graph construction started dominating wall time) the indexed and unindexed versions both measured ~10ms for 200 calls -- the full apply_insertions call's other O(total rules/derived facts) costs (full re-materialisation per stratum, cascade_pd's own build_reverse_index scan) were swamping the one-method difference this issue is actually about. Rewrote the benchmark to bypass stratification entirely: build one DatalogProgram directly from a single rule and inject 300,000 synthetic Derivation entries straight into derived_from under rule_ids that are never looked up (verified safe, since negation_invalidated_seeds only ever indexes into program.rules for the specific rule_id negatively_triggered_rules already matched, never for an unrelated derivation's own rule_id while filtering past it), then time negation_invalidated_seeds directly and repeatedly ahead of a single real apply_insertions call used only for the end-to-end correctness assertion. That isolated benchmark showed the real effect clearly: a temporary revert of just the quads_for_rule call site back to .iter() (confirmed via a baseline run, then restored) measured ~51.4ms/call unindexed vs. ~16.5us/call indexed at NOISE=300,000, roughly 3100x -- numbers recorded in the PR description. Also added unit tests directly on DerivedFromIndex (quads_for_rule_returns_only_matching_rule, quads_for_rule_dedups_and_survives_partial_unrecord, remove_clears_quad_from_every_rule_bucket, duplicate_record_does_not_require_double_unrecord) covering the reverse-index bookkeeping in isolation from the larger reasoner. Full cargo fmt/clippy/test --workspace (plus --no-fail-fast to get full coverage) run to completion: only failure was the known sparql_endpoint::query_timeout OOM SIGKILL false alarm on this memory-constrained (~3.7GB) machine, documented as a known hazard; every other of 187 reported test-result lines across the workspace passed with zero failures." ;
    agp:reasoningFor ghpull:701 ;
    agp:decisionPoint session:pr701Decision ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy session:pr701 ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo session:claudeSonnet5 .

session:pr701Decision a agp:Decision ;
    agp:summaryText "Benchmarked the fix by constructing a single DatalogProgram directly and injecting synthetic Derivation entries straight into derived_from under rule_ids never otherwise referenced, bypassing IncrementalReasoner::new/stratification entirely, so NOISE could reach 300,000 and isolate negation_invalidated_seeds's own cost from unrelated O(rules^2)/O(total derived facts) costs elsewhere in apply_insertions." ;
    agp:alternative "First tried building the benchmark through the full IncrementalReasoner::new -> apply_insertions path with many independent 'noise' rule pairs engineered via a vacuous NOT dependency edge to land in the same stratifier-assigned stratum as the negated rule. Rejected after measuring it empirically: RulePartitioner::new's own O(rules^2) dependency-graph construction made anything past NOISE~2500 impractically slow to set up, and at that scale the full apply_insertions call's other per-round costs (full re-materialisation of every stratum, cascade_pd's reverse-index build) dominated wall time enough that the indexed and unindexed versions measured the same (~10ms/200 calls) -- the difference this issue is about was real but invisible at a scale small enough for setup to stay fast." .
